{"kind":"request","text":"Stop the connector treating staleness as withdrawal, because that makes correct charter replacement disable the connector permanently.\n\nHugh asked, after the #2726 confusion, that any underlying model or projection issue be filed rather than left as a story about one mistake. This is it, and it is a connector defect rather than a kernel one.\n\ninternal/connector/github/read.go:49 refuses a clause when `source.Retired || source.Stale`, and TestRetiredAndStaleClausesDoNotAdmit pins that. Retired is right: a withdrawn clause admits nothing. Stale is not, and the current workroom shows why.\n\nEvery one of hugh's live governance acts for this connector is stale and none is retired: the scoped charter (sequence 2533), the older clause (2459), and the current clause (2666) that names generalbusiness-ai/gitseq#1. They are stale because 2533 says \"Replaces f696c18a\" and f696c18a was retired — which is precisely the correct way to replace a charter. Staleness then propagates to every clause resting on it. So the connector reports \"no live admission clause; nothing to observe\" while looking straight at a live, ratified, correctly scoped charter and a live clause.\n\nThe general shape is worse than this instance. Replacing a charter is the sanctioned governance move, and it makes the successor stale by construction, because the successor must cite what it replaces and what it replaces then gets retired. Under the current rule the sanctioned move is self-defeating: govern correctly and the connector stops. No operator action can escape it, because a charter that cites nothing bounds nothing.\n\nStale means a basis moved, not that the pointer was withdrawn. The connector should admit on liveness — not retired — and surface staleness to the reader instead of silently refusing, so an operator can see that a clause rests on something that has moved and decide whether that matters. The refusal message should also say which clauses were found and why each was rejected; today \"no live admission clause\" is indistinguishable from having none at all, which is what sent me to file a false request against hugh.\n\nThis blocks the outbound half's remaining condition. The end-to-end dogfood needs a scoped outbound charter, and any charter an operator states now will be stale the moment it replaces its predecessor, so the connector would refuse it too.","body":{"conditions":"Admission turns on liveness (not retired) rather than on staleness; a stale-but-live clause admits, and its staleness is reported to the reader rather than silently dropping it; the refusal path names every clause considered and the reason each was rejected, so having no clause is distinguishable from rejecting one; TestRetiredAndStaleClausesDoNotAdmit is replaced by tests that pin retirement as refusing and staleness as admitting-with-notice; the current workroom's charter 2533 and clause 2666 admit under the new rule, demonstrated by a test built from that shape; go vet and make test pass; reviewed by a different agent at an exact head before merge","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}